WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.126
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.126 --> 00:00:07.085
Hi, I'm Rob Siegel,

00:00:07.085 --> 00:00:10.310
lecturer of management at
the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.478
I teach six different courses at the GSB,
but today, we're going to talk about

00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:16.320
the key takeaways from one of them,
the industrialist's dilemma.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.134
[MUSIC]

00:00:21.134 --> 00:00:24.144
What's different about
the industrialist's dilemma is that,

00:00:24.144 --> 00:00:25.621
the combination of physical and

00:00:25.621 --> 00:00:29.500
digital is completely different than
anything that's happened before.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.310
And it's increasing the speed with
which markets are able to change.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.320
And it also enables new companies to end
into markets that previously would not

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.302
have been able to.

00:00:38.302 --> 00:00:42.442
And you can study every industry to see
how the combination of hardware and

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:47.052
software, digital and physical, are being
impacted by these technological and

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:49.060
business model changes.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.528
One of the things we've learned in
studying the industrialist's dilemma is

00:00:52.528 --> 00:00:53.809
that neither incumbents nor

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.210
disruptors are necessarily
preordained to win in the market.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.678
Disruptors have a blank sheet of paper,
they can think with a clean slate about

00:01:00.678 --> 00:01:04.145
how they're going to meet customer needs,
but they often don't have the domain

00:01:04.145 --> 00:01:07.820
knowledge of what it takes to be
successful in a particular market.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.560
Incumbents often have deep balance sheets,
but they need to move more quickly and

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:12.264
build skill and

00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:15.910
competencies that maybe they haven't
had previously in their organization.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.687
[MUSIC]

00:01:17.687 --> 00:01:21.136
In a world where every
product is connected,

00:01:21.136 --> 00:01:24.670
companies need to keep in mind two things.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.244
Number one, they need to develop
the capabilities to write the software to

00:01:28.244 --> 00:01:32.530
communicate with the customers, and then
the how to manage it on an ongoing basis.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.348
To listen to conversations with customers,
to understand how customers are using

00:01:36.348 --> 00:01:39.839
their products, and to think about how
they can constantly improving their

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:43.760
products, to delight customers and
thinking about customer outcomes.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.932
At the same time, organizations need to
understand that the entire ecosystem might

00:01:47.932 --> 00:01:50.107
be jealous of that
ongoing conversation and

00:01:50.107 --> 00:01:54.359
relationship between maybe a company that
manufactures something and the end user.

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:57.714
And people in a channel might want to be
a part of that data stream going back

00:01:57.714 --> 00:01:58.271
and forth.

00:01:58.271 --> 00:02:03.085
[MUSIC]

00:02:03.085 --> 00:02:07.301
In a world where digital and physical
are combining to create massive disruption

00:02:07.301 --> 00:02:10.835
across industries, somebody is
going to end up being the underdog and

00:02:10.835 --> 00:02:13.030
somebody is going to be on their heels.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.840
Being an underdog can
actually be very liberating.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.010
In some ways, when you're on your heels,
you have nothing to lose and

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.060
you have a license to try new things and
be very creative.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.027
You can look at ways in which headwinds
can actually become tailwinds,

00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:28.220
that can actually propel you forward.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.785
So a change in a technology,
a change in an industry, could actually be

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:38.110
an opportunity for a company to catapult
itself to do business in a new way.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.160
An example of a company that
turned a headwind into a tailwind,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.820
is the North American
electronics retailer Best Buy.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.644
Eight years ago their product was
being commoditized, everybody was

00:02:47.644 --> 00:02:52.840
buying televisions, and telephones online
and just trying to get the lowest price.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.261
But as all of these products became
increasingly connected to the internet,

00:02:56.261 --> 00:02:58.370
their level of complexity went up.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.177
What Best Buy figured out is, they could
be the best service retailer not just

00:03:02.177 --> 00:03:06.045
selling the box themselves, but making
sure the customers knew how to connect

00:03:06.045 --> 00:03:09.990
them to the networks and to each other
to deliver a better customer experience.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:14.650
[MUSIC]

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.834
Companies that innovate on
both new technologies and

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:21.850
new business models end up
becoming the winners long term.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.401
A great example is Align Technologies,

00:03:24.401 --> 00:03:28.834
the people who make the clear in
this align teeth straightening tool.

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:32.537
The technology behind
the clear plastic aligners,

00:03:32.537 --> 00:03:35.540
is very difficult to manufacture.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.944
But what they did really well,
is they also increased their channels,

00:03:38.944 --> 00:03:43.200
selling not only through orthodontists but
through dentists, which very much expanded

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.080
the market and allowed adults to start
using the product and not just teenagers.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.363
What we found is that having
business model innovation and

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:53.717
technological innovation,
was a real force multiplier.

00:03:53.717 --> 00:03:58.498
And doing only one of these, was necessary
but not sufficient for long term success.

00:03:58.498 --> 00:04:02.303
[MUSIC]

00:04:02.303 --> 00:04:06.329
As companies from the technology industry
moving to things like health care and

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:10.715
financial services, they start getting in
the areas where governments increasingly

00:04:10.715 --> 00:04:12.320
regulates these areas.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.623
In addition, it starts to get
to national industrial policy.

00:04:14.623 --> 00:04:18.543
Leaders of companies for the next several
decades, are going to have to be very

00:04:18.543 --> 00:04:22.282
conversant in how to work with and
collaborate with governments all over

00:04:22.282 --> 00:04:26.467
the world and know how to be successful
in a world that's increasingly complex.

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:29.320
[MUSIC]

